 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.03 — Surfaces and the First Fundamental Form\n\nA **parametrised surface** is a smooth map\n\n$$\\sigma : U \\to \\mathbb R^3, \\qquad U \\subseteq \\mathbb R^2 \\text{ open},$$\n\nand it is **regular** when $\\sigma_u \\times \\sigma_v \\ne 0$ — the two partial derivatives are independent, so they span a genuine plane. That plane is the **tangent plane** $T_p S$, and the unit normal is\n\n$$n = \\frac{\\sigma_u \\times \\sigma_v}{|\\sigma_u \\times \\sigma_v|}.$$\n\nEverything in this notebook comes from one construction. The surface sits in $\\mathbb R^3$, which has a dot product; restricting that dot product to each tangent plane gives an inner product on $T_pS$, varying from point to point. In the basis $\\{\\sigma_u, \\sigma_v\\}$ it is the symmetric matrix\n\n$$\\mathrm{I} = \\begin{pmatrix} E & F \\\\ F & G \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad E = \\sigma_u\\cdot\\sigma_u, \\quad F = \\sigma_u\\cdot\\sigma_v, \\quad G = \\sigma_v\\cdot\\sigma_v,$$\n\nthe **first fundamental form**, usually written\n\n$$ds^2 = E\\,du^2 + 2F\\,du\\,dv + G\\,dv^2 .$$\n\nThe point of it is this:\n\n> **Everything measurable *within* the surface — lengths of curves, angles between them, areas of regions — is computed from $E, F, G$ alone.**\n\nSuch quantities are called **intrinsic**: an ant confined to the surface, with no access to the surrounding space, could measure them. Quantities that need the ambient $\\mathbb R^3$ — how the surface bends, which way the normal tilts — are **extrinsic**, and belong to 3.04.\n\nThe distinction looks pedantic and is not. In 3.06 we will find that Gaussian curvature, which is *defined* extrinsically from the way the surface curves in space, turns out to be computable from $E, F, G$ alone. That unexpected fact is Gauss's *Theorema Egregium*, and it is why this notebook must come first.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. What the first fundamental form computes\n\n**Length of a curve on the surface.** A curve in the parameter domain, $t \\mapsto (u(t), v(t))$, becomes a curve $\\gamma(t) = \\sigma(u(t), v(t))$ on the surface. By the chain rule $\\gamma' = u'\\sigma_u + v'\\sigma_v$, so\n\n$$|\\gamma'|^2 = E u'^2 + 2F u'v' + G v'^2,$$\n\nand the length is $\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{E u'^2 + 2Fu'v' + Gv'^2}\\;dt$. No $\\sigma$ appears — only $E, F, G$.\n\n**Angle between curves.** If two curves cross with parameter-velocities $(u_1', v_1')$ and $(u_2', v_2')$,\n\n$$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{E u_1'u_2' + F(u_1'v_2' + u_2'v_1') + G v_1'v_2'}{\\sqrt{\\cdots}\\sqrt{\\cdots}}.$$\n\nIn particular the coordinate curves are orthogonal exactly when $F = 0$ — which is why textbook parametrisations so often arrange for that.\n\n**Area.** The parallelogram spanned by $\\sigma_u\\,du$ and $\\sigma_v\\,dv$ has area $|\\sigma_u \\times \\sigma_v|\\,du\\,dv$, and by Lagrange's identity from 1.01,\n\n$$|\\sigma_u\\times\\sigma_v|^2 = |\\sigma_u|^2|\\sigma_v|^2 - (\\sigma_u\\cdot\\sigma_v)^2 = EG - F^2,$$\n\nso\n\n$$\\text{Area} = \\iint \\sqrt{EG - F^2}\\;du\\,dv .$$\n\nThat $\\sqrt{EG-F^2} = \\sqrt{\\det \\mathrm{I}}$ is the determinant-is-a-volume theme from 1.01 once more, and it is the two-dimensional case of the Riemannian volume element $\\sqrt{\\det g}$ that Stage 4 uses throughout. Regularity guarantees $EG - F^2 > 0$.\n\n**Isometries.** Two surfaces are **locally isometric** if they can be parametrised so that their first fundamental forms agree. Then every intrinsic measurement matches — the ant cannot tell them apart. The classic surprise is that a **cylinder is locally isometric to a plane** despite looking curved: rolling a sheet of paper into a tube changes nothing an ant could detect. A sphere, by contrast, is *not* locally isometric to a plane, which is why every flat map of the Earth distorts something.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s, var)  := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\n\n/* The first fundamental form of a parametrised surface */\nFFF(s, u_, v_) := block([su, sv],\n  su : pd(s, u_),  sv : pd(s, v_),\n  matrix([ trigsimp(su . su), trigsimp(su . sv) ],\n         [ trigsimp(su . sv), trigsimp(sv . sv) ]) )$\n\nunitnormal(s, u_, v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  trigsimp( n / sqrt(trigsimp(n . n)) ) )$\n\n/* --- Four surfaces --- */\nassume(R > 0, a > 0, b > 0, cc > 0)$\n\nplane    : [u, v, 0]$\ncylinder : [R*cos(u), R*sin(u), v]$\nsphere   : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$   /* u = colatitude */\ntorus    : [(a + b*cos(u))*cos(v), (a + b*cos(u))*sin(v), b*sin(u)]$\n\nfor su in [[\"plane   \", plane], [\"cylinder\", cylinder],\n           [\"sphere  \", sphere], [\"torus   \", torus]] do block([I1, d],\n  I1 : FFF(su[2], u, v),\n  d  : trigsimp(determinant(I1)),\n  print(su[1], \"  I = \", I1, \"     EG − F² = \", d) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"note F = 0 in every case: these parametrisations use orthogonal\")$\nprint(\"coordinate curves, which is why the matrices are diagonal.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "plane      I =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])      EG − F² =  1\ncylinder   I =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,1])      EG − F² =  R^2\nsphere     I =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])      EG − F² =  R^4*sin(u)^2\ntorus      I =  matrix([b^2,0],[0,b^2*cos(u)^2+2*a*b*cos(u)+a^2])\n             EG − F² =  b^4*cos(u)^2+2*a*b^3*cos(u)+a^2*b^2\nnote F = 0 in every case: these parametrisations use orthogonal\ncoordinate curves, which is why the matrices are diagonal."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The cylinder is locally isometric to the plane --- */\n/* Reparametrise by arc length around the circumference: ũ = R u. */\ncyl_arc : [R*cos(w/R), R*sin(w/R), v]$\nprint(\"cylinder in arc-length coordinates (w = Ru):\")$\nprint(\"   I = \", FFF(cyl_arc, w, v), \"   — identical to the plane's ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so every intrinsic measurement agrees: a sheet of paper rolled into\")$\nprint(\"a tube is unchanged as far as any measurement inside it can tell.\")$\n\n/* --- Lengths on the sphere: a parallel versus a great circle --- */\n/* On the unit sphere, u = colatitude, v = longitude. */\nsph1 : subst(R = 1, sphere)$\nI_sph: FFF(sph1, u, v)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"unit sphere: I = \", I_sph, \"   so ds² = du² + sin²u dv²\")$\n\n/* the parallel at colatitude u0, all the way round: u' = 0, v' = 1 */\nlen_parallel(u0) := integrate( sqrt( subst(u = u0, I_sph[2][2]) ), v, 0, 2*%pi )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"length of the parallel at colatitude u₀ = \", len_parallel(u0))$\nprint(\"   at the equator u₀ = π/2 : \", len_parallel(%pi/2), \" = 2π ✓\")$\nprint(\"   at u₀ = π/6             : \", len_parallel(%pi/6), \" = π  (a small circle)\")$\n\n/* the meridian from pole to pole: u' = 1, v' = 0 */\nprint(\"   length of a meridian    = \",\n      integrate( sqrt(I_sph[1][1]), u, 0, %pi ), \" = π ✓\")$\n\n/* --- Area of the sphere --- */\nareaform(s, u_, v_) := trigsimp( sqrt( trigsimp(determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_))) ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"area element of the sphere: √(EG−F²) = \", areaform(sphere, u, v))$\nprint(\"total area = \", integrate( integrate( R^2*sin(u), u, 0, %pi ), v, 0, 2*%pi ),\n      \"   = 4πR² ✓\")$\n\n/* --- Area of the torus --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"torus area element = \", areaform(torus, u, v))$\nprint(\"total area = \",\n      integrate( integrate( b*(a + b*cos(u)), u, 0, 2*%pi ), v, 0, 2*%pi ),\n      \"   = (2πa)(2πb) — Pappus' theorem ✓\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "cylinder in arc-length coordinates (w = Ru):\n   I =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])    — identical to the plane's ✓\nso every intrinsic measurement agrees: a sheet of paper rolled into\na tube is unchanged as far as any measurement inside it can tell.\nunit sphere: I =  matrix([1,0],[0,sin(u)^2])    so ds² = du² + sin²u dv²\nlength of the parallel at colatitude u₀ =  2*%pi*abs(sin(u0))\n   at the equator u₀ = π/2 :  2*%pi  = 2π ✓\n   at u₀ = π/6             :  %pi  = π  (a small circle)\n   length of a meridian    =  %pi  = π ✓\narea element of the sphere: √(EG−F²) =  R^2*abs(sin(u))\ntotal area =  4*%pi*R^2    = 4πR² ✓\ntorus area element =  sqrt(b^4*cos(u)^2+2*a*b^3*cos(u)+a^2*b^2)\ntotal area =  4*%pi^2*a*b    = (2πa)(2πb) — Pappus' theorem ✓"
     ]
    }
   ]
