  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Conformal is not isometric: why maps of the Earth lie\n\nThe sphere is *not* locally isometric to the plane. Proving that needs the Theorema Egregium (3.06), so we take it on trust for one more notebook. But it is worth seeing exactly how close one can get, because the near-miss is what cartography lives on.\n\nStart from the sphere's form $ds^2 = du^2 + \\sin^2\\!u\\;dv^2$ and change the colatitude coordinate to\n\n$$w = \\ln \\tan\\frac u2, \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad du = \\sin u\\,dw .$$\n\nSubstituting,\n\n$$ds^2 = \\sin^2\\!u\\,\\bigl(dw^2 + dv^2\\bigr).$$\n\nThe first fundamental form has become a **scalar multiple of the flat one**. A form $\\lambda(u,v)^2(dw^2+dv^2)$ is called **conformally flat**, and because angles depend only on ratios in the formula of §1, the scalar cancels:\n\n> **Mercator's projection preserves angles exactly, and distances not at all.**\n\nThat is precisely what a navigator wants — a constant compass bearing is a straight line on the chart — and precisely what a geographer does not, since the area scale factor is $\\sin^2 u$, growing without bound towards the poles. Greenland is not the size of Africa.\n\nThe obstruction is genuine: no substitution can make the factor $1$, because a conformal factor cannot be removed when the curvature is nonzero. Compare 2.05, where the Möbius maps were conformal for the same underlying reason — holomorphic maps scale the metric without twisting it — and 2.06, where the hyperbolic models were conformal so that *drawn* angles were correct while drawn distances were not. Every model of a curved geometry in a flat picture makes this trade.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Angles from the first fundamental form --- */\n/* cos θ between parameter-velocities p and q, using I */\nangle_I(I1, p, q) := block([num, n1, n2],\n  num : I1[1][1]*p[1]*q[1] + I1[1][2]*(p[1]*q[2] + p[2]*q[1]) + I1[2][2]*p[2]*q[2],\n  n1  : sqrt( I1[1][1]*p[1]^2 + 2*I1[1][2]*p[1]*p[2] + I1[2][2]*p[2]^2 ),\n  n2  : sqrt( I1[1][1]*q[1]^2 + 2*I1[1][2]*q[1]*q[2] + I1[2][2]*q[2]^2 ),\n  trigsimp( num/(n1*n2) ) )$\n\nprint(\"on the sphere, the coordinate curves meet at cos θ = \",\n      angle_I(I_sph, [1,0], [0,1]), \"  — orthogonal, since F = 0\")$\n\n/* A curve crossing a meridian at 45° in parameter space is NOT at 45° on\n   the surface, because E ≠ G. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"parameter direction (1,1) against the meridian (1,0):\")$\nprint(\"   cos θ = \", angle_I(I_sph, [1,1], [1,0]))$\nprint(\"   at colatitude u = π/2 : θ = \",\n      float( acos(subst(u=%pi/2, angle_I(I_sph,[1,1],[1,0]))) * 180/%pi ), \"°\")$\nprint(\"   at colatitude u = π/6 : θ = \",\n      float( acos(subst(u=%pi/6, angle_I(I_sph,[1,1],[1,0]))) * 180/%pi ), \"°\")$\nprint(\"   — the same parameter direction, different true angles\")$\n\n/* --- The Mercator substitution makes I conformally flat --- */\n/* w = ln tan(u/2)  ⟹  u = 2 atan(e^w),  and we need du/dw = sin u.\n   trigsimp alone will not reduce sin(2 atan x); expand it first. */\nu_of_w : 2*atan(exp(w))$\nlhs_du : ratsimp( diff(u_of_w, w) )$\nrhs_su : ratsimp( trigexpand( sin(u_of_w) ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Mercator: u = 2 arctan(e^w)\")$\nprint(\"   du/dw            = \", lhs_du)$\nprint(\"   sin(u), expanded = \", rhs_su)$\nprint(\"   residual         = \", ratsimp(lhs_du - rhs_su), \"   — equal ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so ds² = du² + sin²u dv² = sin²u (dw² + dv²): conformally flat,\")$\nprint(\"with conformal factor λ = sin u, and area scaled by sin²u.\")$\n\n/* Area distortion at various latitudes */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   latitude    colatitude u    inflation 1/sin²u\")$\nfor lat in [0, 30, 45, 60, 75, 85] do block([uu],\n  uu : float((90 - lat)*%pi/180),\n  print(\"   \", lat, \"°          \", uu, \"        \", float(1/sin(uu)^2)) )$\nprint(\"   — how much a region is INFLATED on the Mercator chart\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "on the sphere, the coordinate curves meet at cos θ =\n  0   — orthogonal, since F = 0\nparameter direction (1,1) against the meridian (1,0):\n   cos θ =  1/sqrt(sin(u)^2+1)\n   at colatitude u = π/2 : θ =  45.0 °\n   at colatitude u = π/6 : θ =  26.565051177077994 °\n   — the same parameter direction, different true angles\nMercator: u = 2 arctan(e^w)\n   du/dw            =  (2*%e^w)/(%e^(2*w)+1)\n   sin(u), expanded =  (2*%e^w)/(%e^(2*w)+1)\n   residual         =  0    — equal ✓\nso ds² = du² + sin²u dv² = sin²u (dw² + dv²): conformally flat,\nwith conformal factor λ = sin u, and area scaled by sin²u.\n   latitude    colatitude u    inflation 1/sin²u\n    0 °           1.5707963267948966          1.0\n    30 °           1.0471975511965976          1.3333333333333335\n    45 °           0.7853981633974483          2.0000000000000004\n    60 °           0.5235987755982988          4.000000000000001\n    75 °           0.2617993877991494          14.928203230275512\n    85 °           0.08726646259971647          131.6460956438599\n   — how much a region is INFLATED on the Mercator chart"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Mercator area inflation as a function of latitude, with some landmarks */\nlats : makelist(float(k), k, 0, 84)$\ninfl(lat) := float( 1/sin((90-lat)*%pi/180)^2 )$\n\nprint(\"Greenland sits near 72°N, the Democratic Republic of the Congo near 0°.\")$\nprint(\"   inflation at 72° = \", infl(72), \"     at 0° = \", infl(0))$\nprint(\"   ratio            = \", infl(72)/infl(0),\n      \"  — which is why Greenland looks continent-sized\")$\n\nplot2d([ [discrete, lats, map(infl, lats)],\n         [discrete, [0.0, 84.0], [1.0, 1.0]] ],\n       [x, 0, 85], [y, 0, 40],\n       [style, [lines,3],[lines,1]],\n       [legend, \"area inflation 1/sin²u\", \"true scale\"],\n       [xlabel, \"latitude (degrees)\"], [ylabel, \"area magnification\"],\n       [title, \"Mercator preserves every angle and almost no area\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Greenland sits near 72°N, the Democratic Republic of the Congo near 0°.\n   inflation at 72° =  10.472135954999581      at 0° =  1.0\n   ratio            =  10.472135954999581\n                        — which is why Greenland looks continent-sized"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/svg+xml": [
