   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Surface | $\\mathrm{I} = \\begin{pmatrix}E&F\\\\F&G\\end{pmatrix}$ | $EG-F^2$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| plane | $\\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$ | $1$ |\n| cylinder radius $R$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(R^2,1)$ | $R^2$ |\n| cylinder, arc-length coords | $\\mathrm{diag}(1,1)$ | $1$ |\n| sphere radius $R$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(R^2,\\,R^2\\sin^2 u)$ | $R^4\\sin^2 u$ |\n| torus $(a,b)$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(b^2,\\,(a+b\\cos u)^2)$ | $b^2(a+b\\cos u)^2$ |\n\nComputed from these alone: lengths of parallels ($2\\pi\\sin u_0$) and meridians ($\\pi$) on the unit sphere; the area of the sphere ($4\\pi R^2$) and of the torus ($4\\pi^2ab$, Pappus's theorem); angles, which vary with position even for a fixed parameter direction; and the fact that a suitable change of coordinates makes the cylinder's form **literally identical** to the plane's.\n\n## The idea to hold on to\n\n**Intrinsic versus extrinsic.** The first fundamental form is the complete record of what can be measured *inside* a surface. The cylinder demonstrates that this is strictly less than what can be seen from outside: it is visibly curved in $\\mathbb R^3$, yet intrinsically indistinguishable from a flat plane. Rolling paper is an isometry; crumpling it is not.\n\nThe sphere is the opposite case — no reparametrisation flattens it, and the best available compromise is *conformal*: Mercator gets every angle right and inflates area by $1/\\sin^2 u$, a factor of about $10.5$ at Greenland's latitude. That trade-off is unavoidable, and its unavoidability is a theorem about curvature rather than a failure of cartographic ingenuity.\n\n**Where this goes.** The pattern\n\n$$\\text{restrict the ambient inner product to each tangent space}$$\n\nis the entire definition of a **Riemannian metric**, and Stage 4 simply drops the ambient space, keeping the varying inner product as the primitive object. Then $E, F, G$ become $g_{ij}$, $\\sqrt{EG-F^2}$ becomes $\\sqrt{\\det g}$, and everything computed in this notebook has a verbatim counterpart. The surfaces here are the case $n = 2$ with the metric inherited rather than postulated.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.04 — The Gauss Map and Second Fundamental Form** turns to the extrinsic side: how the unit normal $n$ tilts as you move across the surface. Its derivative is the shape operator, a *symmetric* linear map on each tangent plane — so by 1.06 it can be diagonalised, and its eigenvalues are the principal curvatures.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §2-5 for the first fundamental form; §4-2 for isometries and the cylinder example.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapters 4–6, with many worked first fundamental forms.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 2 introduces Riemannian metrics as exactly this construction with the ambient space removed.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 5 for the metric on a surface and the transition to the general case.\n\nOn map projections and the impossibility of a distortion-free chart, see Snyder, J.P. (1987) *Map Projections: A Working Manual*. USGS Professional Paper 1395.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}